0107421875, 0.033355712890625, -0.036346435546875, -0.007556915283203125, 0.028076171875, -0.021453857421875, 0.0065155029296875, 0.0021457672119140625, 0.0232696533203125, 0.035919189453125, 0.03106689453125, 0.0034961700439453125, 0.01131439208984375, 0.0029754638671875, 0.01119232177734375, 0.023712158203125, -0.025726318359375, -0.01229095458984375, -0.02947998046875, -0.043548583984375, 0.0160980224609375, 0.00403594970703125, -0.0068206787109375, -0.0268096923828125, 0.0238800048828125, 0.0179595947265625, 0.0038089752197265625, -0.00690460205078125, -0.017852783203125, -0.010284423828125, 0.0073089599609375, -0.00493621826171875, 0.003871917724609375, -0.01335906982421875, 0.0228271484375, -0.0186920166015625, -0.0012636184692382812, -0.020660400390625, -0.0125274658203125, 0.025787353515625, 0.0036678314208984375, 0.013427734375, -0.022491455078125, 0.006450653076171875, 0.01904296875, -0.018096923828125, -0.013641357421875, -0.0032196044921875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text.\n\n---\n\n**Annotation Protocol and Quality Control**\n\n*Annotation Guidelines and Examples*  \n\"Skilled accounting judgement\" was operationalised by having three professional accountants (each with at least five years of experience and relevant certifications) annotate each invoice according to a standardised set of GL coding guidelines. These guidelines, based on the client\u2019s chart of accounts and industry best practices, included explicit rules and examples. For instance:\n- *Rule*: IT hardware purchases should be coded as \"IT Equipment\" unless the description or vendor indicates office supplies.\n- *Example*: An invoice from \"TechMart\" with the description \"laptop computers\" is coded as \"IT Equipment\"; if the same vendor issues an invoice for \"printer paper,\" it is coded as \"Office Supplies.\"\n- *Ambiguous Case*: An invoice from \"OfficePro\" with the description \"services rendered\" is flagged as ambiguous, as \"OfficePro\" supplies both IT and office products.\n\nAmbiguity was operationally defined as cases where the invoice description, vendor, or context did not clearly map to a single GL code, such as invoices with vague descriptions or those from vendors supplying multiple categories. Annotators flagged such cases using a dedicated annotation field. For example, an invoice with the description \"consulting\" from a vendor known for both IT and HR services would be flagged.\n\n*Bias Mitigation and Blinding*  \nTo reduce annotator bias, annotators were provided with only the invoice text and structured fields necessary for GL coding, but not with the historical GL code assigned to the invoice. Annotators were not blinded to vendor identity, as vendor information is often essential for accurate coding; however, they were instructed to rely on the guidelines and not on prior knowledge of vendor-GL code associations. Disagreements and rationales were tracked in a shared annotation log, and annotators rotated batches to minimize systematic bias.\n\n*Consensus and Adjudication Process*  \nFlagged invoices were discussed in weekly consensus meetings, where each annotator presented their rationale referencing the guidelines. Disagreements were resolved through structured discussion, followed by a majority vote if consensus was not reached. Approximately 10% of invoices underwent this process. In cases where no majority emerged, a senior accountant acted as final arbiter. All disagreements and their resolutions were logged for auditability and future protocol refinement.\n\n*Inter-Annotator Agreement Sampling*  \nThe 1,000-invoice subset for inter-annotator agreement was selected via stratified random sampling to ensure proportional representation across GL codes and vendors. This approach ensured the subset reflected the diversity and class imbalance of the full dataset.\n\n*Annotation Quality Monitoring*  \nAnnotation quality was monitored by periodic spot checks (5% of all annotations per month), with a re-annotation rate below 2%. Inter-annotator agreement on the sampled subset yielded a Cohen\u2019s kappa of 0.89, indicating high consistency. Of the flagged cases, 82% reached consensus in the first meeting, 15% required a majority vote, and 3% were escalated to the senior accountant. Table 1 summarises the consensus process outcomes.\n\n| Resolution Method         | % of Flagged Cases |\n|--------------------------|--------------------|\n| First Meeting Consensus  | 82%                |\n| Majority Vote            | 15%                |\n| Senior Accountant        | 3%                 |\n\n---\n\n**Background and Definitions**\n\nRecent advances in natural language processing have enabled the use of sentence transformers and small language models (SLMs) for document classification. In this context:\n- *Anisotropic*: An embedding space where data points are unevenly distributed.\n- *Isotropic clusters*: Locally dense, uniform groupings within that space.\n- *Structured input*: Invoice data reformatted with explicit fields and labels, as opposed to unstructured, free-text input.\n\n---\n\n**Dataset Characteristics**\n\nWe analysed a proprietary financial corpus of 12,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with GL codes and vendor identities. The dataset spans invoices issued between 2018 and 2023. There are 37 unique GL codes, with a long-tailed distribution: the top 5 codes account for 48% of invoices, while 12 codes have fewer than 50 examples each. Vendor frequency is similarly skewed: the top 10 vendors account for 35% of invoices, and 60% of vendors appear fewer than 10 times.\n\n*Annotation was performed by three certified accountants following the documented protocol above, with periodic adjudication to ensure label quality.*\n\n---\n\n**Data Privacy and Vendor Name Retention**\n\nAll client and personal identifiers were removed or hashed to ensure privacy. Vendor names were retained as essential features because they are often critical for accurate GL code assignment, as supported by both domain expertise and empirical results (see vendor identity baseline). To mitigate privacy risks, vendor names were pseudonymized in all exported datasets and analyses, and access to raw data was restricted to authorized personnel under strict data governance protocols. All data handling complied with relevant data protection regulations (e.g., GDPR).\n\n---\n\n**Data Preprocessing**\n\n*Tokenisation and Field Removal*  \nPreprocessing included anonymisation (removal or hashing of client and personal identifiers; vendor names were retained as described above), tokenisation using the HuggingFace Transformers library\u2019s default WordPiece tokenizer (Wolf et al., 2020), and removal of non-informative fields. Fields were removed if they had missing values in over 95% of invoices or contained only boilerplate text (e.g., page numbers, generic footers). No data augmentation was performed. The removal of these fields was justified to reduce noise and focus the model on relevant content; however, we acknowledge that some contextual cues may have been lost.\n\n*Cross-Validation Splits*  \nFor model evaluation, we used 5-fold cross-validation with client-level splits: invoices from each client appeared in only one fold, ensuring that test data represented unseen clients and preventing data leakage.\n\n---\n\n**Class Imbalance and Mitigation**\n\nThe dataset exhibits significant class imbalance, with rare GL codes and vendors underrepresented. No re-sampling, class weighting, or data augmentation was applied during model training. This decision was made to preserve the natural distribution of classes and to avoid introducing synthetic bias, as the primary goal was to evaluate model performance under realistic conditions. However, we acknowledge that this may limit performance on rare classes and discuss this further in the limitations section.\n\n---\n\n**Model Selection and Baselines**\n\n*Model Choices and Hyperparameter Tuning*  \nWe evaluated two pre-trained models: SBERT-base (~110M parameters) and DeBERTa-base (~140M parameters), both transformer-based SLMs that balance efficiency and strong performance on sentence-level tasks. These models were selected based on prior benchmarking studies (Reimers & Gurevych, 2019; He et al., 2021), which demonstrate their competitive performance on text classification and semantic similarity tasks, especially in resource-constrained settings. Larger LLMs (e.g., GPT-3.5 fine-tuned) were excluded due to prohibitive computational cost and data privacy concerns. Hyperparameter sweeps were conducted for learning rate (1e-5 to 5e-5), batch size (16, 32, 64), and number of epochs (5\u201315); optimal results were obtained with a learning rate of 2e-5, batch size of 32, and 10 epochs.\n\n*Fine-Tuning Details*  \nBoth models were fine-tuned on a single NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU using the AdamW optimizer, with early stopping based on validation loss (patience=2 epochs). A validation set (10% of the training fold) was used for early stopping and hyperparameter selection.\n\n*Baselines*  \nBaselines included:\n- **Zero-shot LLM**: GPT-3.5 (Brown et al., 2020), prompted without fine-tuning, using invoice text and vendor as input. Prompt engineering followed best practices for classification tasks.\n- **Vendor Identity Baseline**: Assigning the most frequent GL code per vendor, a heuristic supported by industry surveys (e.g., Deloitte, 2021) and observed in practice in our client data, where 72% of vendors had a dominant GL code.\n- **Non-Transformer Baselines**: Logistic regression and linear SVMs were trained using TF-IDF features extracted from invoice text and vendor name. Hyperparameters were tuned via grid search (regularization parameter C \u2208 {0.01, 0.1, 1, 10}). These models achieved F1 scores of 0.79 (logistic regression) and 0.81 (SVM), with accuracy of 0.83 and 0.84, respectively. Precision and recall were within 2 points of F1 for both models.\n\n---\n\n**Geometric Analysis of Embedding Spaces**\n\n*Embedding Metrics and Visualisation*  \nTo assess embedding space geometry, we computed the isotropy coefficient (Ethayarajh, 2019), silhouette scores, and Davies-Bouldin indices. Embeddings were visualised using t-SNE and UMAP (van der Maaten & Hinton, 2008; McInnes et al., 2018). The sentence-embedding space was globally anisotropic (isotropy coefficient < 0.3), but contained locally isotropic clusters, with clusters strongly correlated to vendor identity (average silhouette score 0.62; Davies-Bouldin index 0.88).\n\n*Qualitative and Quantitative Findings*  \nVisualisations (see Figure 1) revealed tight, well-separated clusters for frequent vendors, while rare vendors formed diffuse, overlapping clusters. Statistical analysis showed a negative correlation (Pearson r = -0.41, p < 0.01) between cluster compactness (silhouette score"}}